Functional Diarrhea
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Painless recurrent loose/watery stools, Absence of nocturnal diarrhoea, Absence of blood/steatorrhoea, Normal colonoscopy with random biopsies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-015
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of profuse, watery diarrhea causing severe anxiety regarding impending dehydration and inability to leave the house.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated highly frequent, watery diarrhea leading to acute patient distress and fear of severe dehydration. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively assess clinical hydration status, rule out acute infectious dysentery or inflammatory bowel disease flares, and initiate safe, immediate anti-diarrheal therapy to restore functional status before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've had to run to the toilet ten times today with complete liquid, I'm terrified I'm going to severely dehydrate, I can't even leave my bedroom."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 24-hour acute escalation of continuous, completely watery stools (Bristol Stool Form Scale type 7). This occurs on a background of recurrent loose stools over the past three months. The patient explicitly notes a complete absence of severe, predominant abdominal pain or intense cramping. There is a complete absence of visible gross blood, significant mucus, or greasy steatorrhoea. The patient does not experience nocturnal awakenings due to diarrhea. Symptoms are associated with severe urgency and a feeling of an impending accident. Normal baseline appetite.
Functional Status: Housebound strictly due to the extreme urgency and frequency of bowel movements.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high anxiety regarding dehydration and absolute loss of bowel control.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Anxious, frequently looking towards the bathroom, well-perfused.
Physical Exam: General appearance shows no signs of severe clinical dehydration; mucous membranes are moist, capillary refill is less than 2 seconds, and skin turgor is completely normal. Abdominal examination reveals a soft, non-tender abdomen with hyperactive bowel sounds. Complete absence of an acute palpable abdominal mass, localized tenderness, or peritonism. Documented history of a completely normal colonoscopy with normal random mucosal biopsies, and negative coeliac serology.
Environmental Observations: Patient has an untouched meal on the counter, afraid to eat due to immediate gastrocolic reflex triggering diarrhea.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil recent antibiotic use; nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Functional Diarrhea - Status: Acute hypermotility flare
Differentials:

Infectious Gastroenteritis/Dysentery - Ruled out due to complete absence of fever, severe cramping, bloody stool, or sick contacts.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) flare - Ruled out clinically by the absence of severe pain, absence of hematochezia, and previously documented normal colonoscopy/biopsies.

Microscopic Colitis - Ruled out explicitly by the documented normal random mucosal biopsies from a previous colonoscopy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe hypermotility flare of functional diarrhea, causing acute distress, extreme urgency, and a palpable fear of severe dehydration. An immediate, hands-on clinical examination was necessary to formally assess the patient's objective hydration status and definitively exclude acute infectious dysentery or a severe inflammatory bowel disease flare via abdominal palpation and clinical history. Establishing clinical euvolemia and ruling out acute pathology allowed for immediate reassurance and the safe prescription of acute anti-motility agents, successfully preventing a high-anxiety emergency department presentation. This fulfills the strict criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Loperamide 4mg stat to immediately slow colonic transit time and reduce urgency. Prescribed Loperamide 2mg PRN after each loose stool (maximum 16mg/day) strictly for the next 48 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of avoiding all food entirely, eat small, low-residue, soluble-fiber meals (e.g., plain white rice, toast) throughout the day. By consuming these binding foods, the patient will physically bulk the stool, directly reducing the watery consistency and rewarding the patient with a return of bowel confidence.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to discuss dietary optimization (e.g., low FODMAP trial) if loose stools persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing high fevers, severe, continuous abdominal pain, passing bright red blood or black tarry stools, or becoming completely unable to urinate for 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute management is based on the absence of infectious signs; if fevers or bleeding develop, stool cultures will be immediately required.
Clinical Course: Functional diarrhea is a benign hypermotility condition. It does not lead to malnutrition or permanent bowel damage. Acute flares are rapidly manageable with simple anti-motility medications and dietary adjustments.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, start passing visible blood or black, sticky stools, or if you stop passing urine entirely.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel is currently contracting too quickly, not allowing enough time for water to be absorbed. The medication (Loperamide) acts like a brake pedal for the bowel, slowing the transit time to allow water absorption and instantly stopping the urgency.
Actionable Steps: Take the anti-motility medication immediately after the next loose bowel movement. Experiencing the rapid cessation of urgency will immediately decrease your anxiety, reinforcing the use of the medication over simply avoiding food and water.

